/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_configure Configure ExaGRyPE

@image html tutorials/exahype2/exagrype/arty.png

ExaGRyPE needs to be installed as an application of the Peano framework. For general remarks on
how to install Peano, please see \ref page_installation_home. This tutorial is also designed to be
self-contained, and thus should include all the steps you need to run the example code.

The code is still under active development in the repository at branch application-ccz4
(the name, which is not a good one, is subject to future change). To start, please clone
Peano and switch to the branch

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
git clone https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano.git
git checkout applications-ccz4
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

then configure and compile Peano under the main directory as follows (please make sure that you load
the proper modules if you are working on a cluster such as COSMA, see \ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_clusters)

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .; automake --add-missing
./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx --with-mpi=mpiicpx --with-multithreading=omp CXXFLAGS="-Ofast -std=c++20 -qopenmp -march=native -mtune=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -Wno-attributes=clang:: -w" LDFLAGS="-qopenmp" --enable-loadbalancing --enable-exahype --enable-exagrype --with-gsl --enable-particles --enable-blockstructured --enable-finiteelements
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The flag \c --enable-exagrype (\c -DENABLE_EXAGRYPE=ON if you use CMake) builds the ExaGRyPE
library, which hosts the CCZ4 kernels and the analytical initial conditions (see \ref page_exagrype_library).
It requires ExaHyPE 2 and is built for 3d only. Without it, the generated ExaGRyPE Makefiles will
not link. The black hole scenarios additionally need \c --with-gsl (\c -DWITH_GSL=ON), as
their TwoPunctures initial data relies on the GNU Scientific Library.

Missing certain installed libraries may lead to configuration/compilation errors; please check
\ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_faq if necessary. Note you only need to compile Peano
once as long as your working environments and Peano kernel code are unchanged. And we recommend 
re-compiling it after you pull a new code version or change working environments (e.g., changing 
between different login nodes of a cluster)

The next step is to set up the Python environment. ExaGRyPE's code generation relies on
several Python packages; the recommended way to install them is an editable install from
the Peano root directory (where the \c pyproject.toml file resides). This installs the full
set of required dependencies and sets the Python path to the Peano modules automatically,
so there is no need to export \c PYTHONPATH by hand. For the complete dependency list and
alternative setups (e.g. setting \c PYTHONPATH manually), see \ref page_installation_home.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -e .
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

With the environment in place, enter the ExaGRyPE subdirectory:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cd ./applications/exahype2/ccz4/
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To clean the repository completely, use the following lines

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm -rf aclocal.m4 compile src/config.* configure *sh Makefile Makefile.in missing stamp* ar-lib autom4te.cache *.log libtool depcomp *.scan
find . -name Makefile -delete
find . -name Makefile.in -delete
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


# Reference papers

The following paper contains the physics and code design of ExaGRyPE.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
@article{Zhang:2024:ExaGRyPE,
title = {ExaGRyPE: Numerical general relativity solvers based upon the hyperbolic PDEs solver engine ExaHyPE},
journal = {Computer Physics Communications},
volume = {307},
pages = {109435},
year = {2025},
issn = {0010-4655},
doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2024.109435},
url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0010465524003588},
author = {Han Zhang and Baojiu Li and Tobias Weinzierl and Cristian Barrera-Hinojosa},
keywords = {Software design, Numerical relativity, Hyperbolic partial differential equations, Finite Differences, Adaptive mesh refinement, Domain decomposition, Task parallelism},
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

*/
